I'm afraid I'm not very progressive about wishing - the way it is now seems to
work okay, so why change it?

I did want to point out something, though - NetHack would IMHO be a total
travesty if, for the sake of wishing, it had to include a full-blown Natural
Language Parser (NLP). Just ask some of the dudes over in comp.ai.* how much
code that would take in C. I think it would easily add enough to make the
game unrunnable on PC's and probably UNIXPC's. It'd probably be pretty tough
for low-end workstations. And every class of computer, perhaps short of a
Cray, would be noticeably impacted.

So much for alarmism.

Fact is, wishing is difficult. On the one hand, I don't think we want to just
parade every possibly-useful item in the whole game in front of the player,
and have him or her make a menu choice. That is (as some people have pointed
out) not the way wishes have been understood to work in traditional mythology.

On the other hand, we don't want to unfairly penalize the player for a simple 
fat-fingered spelling mistake, either.

So, what we need are creative suggestions for modest improvements on the
current wishing mechanism, not just complaints that some wishes don't work,
or give unexpected results.

Dave Hairston has suggested a tree-based wishing scheme. Unfortunately, I 
think it gives away too much. But at least it is creative. Please keep them
coming...
